{
  "title": "Move-In Checklist: Printable Condition Form",
  "metaDescription": "Create a customized, printable move-in checklist for your rental. Walk each room, track fixture conditions, and compare side by side at move-out.",
  "h1": "Move-In Checklist for Renters and Landlords",
  "introText": "On a move-in checklist, you should write down the baseline condition of every wall, floor, window, fixture, and appliance in each room on the day you get the keys. Walking each room on move-in day to record existing marks, cracks, or operational issues with dated photographs provides a clear record for both tenant and landlord. That shared document serves as the reference point at move-out, because comparing those notes against current conditions determines any security deposit deductions.\n\nOur interactive tool lets you assemble a custom inspection sheet for your specific rental layout in seconds. You select your role as tenant or landlord, pick your property type, and enter your exact counts for bedrooms and bathrooms. The tool instantly drops rooms and features that do not apply, such as a garage or yard, so you only inspect spaces your rental actually has. When you finish customizing the sheet, click the Print or save as PDF button to produce a clean paper worksheet complete with property address lines and signature blocks.",
  "toolHeading": "Build your move-in checklist",
  "toolSubheading": "Select your property details to generate a room-by-room condition worksheet.",
  "howItWorks": "A move-in condition form establishes a shared factual baseline before anyone unpacks furniture. Both tenant and landlord benefit from using the exact same document, because neither party has to rely on unwritten memory months or years later. When both sides inspect the rental together and sign off on the same sheet, disagreements over pre-existing scuffs or damaged blinds drop significantly. The completed form gives everyone a neutral record of how the home looked the moment possession changed hands.\n\nFilling out the inspection form works best when you record specific, plain descriptions rather than broad terms. Write detailed notes like small scratch near front lock, hairline crack in window pane, or clean condition across all surfaces. Take clear, dated photographs of any noted damage, and back those photos up alongside your written notes. Check every fixture during your walk, including turning on faucets to observe drainage and running stove burners. Test safety hardware during the walkthrough as well; the [National Fire Protection Association](https://www.nfpa.org/education-and-research/home-fire-safety/smoke-alarms) advises having smoke alarms inside every bedroom, outside each sleeping area, and on every level, testing them monthly, and replacing them every 10 years. For carbon monoxide alarms, the [Consumer Product Safety Commission](https://www.cpsc.gov/Safety-Education/Safety-Education-Centers/Carbon-Monoxide-Information-Center) advises installing alarms on each level of the home and outside sleeping areas.\n\nThe tool above tailors the checklist layout to the rooms and amenities you choose. In our internal comparison, we ran the rules through the tool for two typical rental homes to evaluate the resulting worksheets. A compact studio apartment produces 5 rooms and 36 items to inspect, covering the entry, main room, kitchen, bathroom, and utility systems. A 3-bedroom, 2-bathroom house with an in-unit washer and dryer, dishwasher, garage, and yard produces 13 rooms and 89 items. Selecting the right options eliminates clutter so you never waste time scanning checklist rows for features your home lacks.\n\nWhen lease termination arrives, you pull out this identical form to complete the move-out inspection column. Comparing the move-in notes directly against the move-out column shows whether marks reflect pre-existing flaws or fresh tenant damage. Rules governing security deposit deductions, allowed repair charges, and deposit return deadlines are determined by state and local laws. Some state statutes require landlords to provide a written move-in condition checklist to hold any deposit funds. You can look up your state regulations through the [Department of Housing and Urban Development](https://www.hud.gov/topics/rental_assistance/tenantrights) tenant rights directory.\n\nTenants and landlords each have specific priorities to remember during the walkthrough. If you are a renter preparing for lease signing, our [renting checklist](/checklists/renting-checklist/) and [first apartment checklist](/checklists/first-apartment-checklist/) outline your broader planning steps, including utility setups. For property owners and managers, our [landlord checklist](/checklists/landlord-checklist/) highlights documentation duties and maintenance routines. Tenants should focus on logging every small surface blemish before moving heavy furniture inside. Landlords should ensure all fixtures function properly and confirm that safety alarms pass standard operational tests.\n\nYour practical takeaway is simple: complete the full walkthrough on day one, sign the document together, and retain separate copies. Both tenant and landlord should keep their signed form and photo files in a safe place until the lease ends. When moving day arrives at the end of tenancy, walk the rental again with this same move in checklist to confirm final property conditions together.",
  "workedExample": "At The Modern Wallet, we evaluated how property specifications alter inspection workloads by testing our checklist logic on two distinct rental profiles. In our internal comparison, we ran the checklist's rules for two example homes to count the active fields generated for each property layout. The first scenario represented a single tenant leasing an urban studio apartment without exterior amenities. The second scenario modeled a family renting a suburban single-family house with multiple bedrooms, laundry machines, and outdoor grounds.\n\nOur rules produced 5 rooms and 36 items to record for the studio apartment, keeping the inspection focused on core living areas. The 3-bedroom, 2-bathroom house with laundry, dishwasher, garage, and yard produced 13 rooms and 89 items to record. This comparison verified that dynamic checklists remove dozens of irrelevant items for smaller apartments while keeping necessary outdoor, garage, and multi-bedroom checks in place for larger properties.",
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Relying on vague words like fair or normal instead of describing exact marks, chipped paint, or cracked tiles.",
    "Skipping dated photographs of pre-existing flaws that support your written condition notes.",
    "Failing to test basic operational elements such as window latches, water pressure, stove burners, and safety alarms.",
    "Leaving the form unsigned by either tenant or landlord at the end of the walkthrough.",
    "Losing the move-in document before the lease ends, leaving you without evidence during the move-out inspection.",
    "Assuming state deposit return rules match neighboring areas instead of checking state law."
  ],
